-- title:
--   Degeneracy maps vanish on toric p-power points
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and an integer $M \ge 1$ with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$, a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$, and a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying over $p$ in the sense that $p$ is a non-unit of $A$, whose residue field is algebraically closed of characteristic $p$. Let $\Lambda$ be level data at $(p, M, H)$ over $A$: a structure morphism $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ from $\operatorname{Spec} A$ to $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the ring of rationals with denominator coprime to $p$ with $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ pulled back along the generic point equal to the generic point, a scheme $\Lambda.X$ with a morphism $\Lambda.f$ to that base, a relative group law $\Lambda.L$ on $\Lambda.f$, a bijection $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}$ from $J_{H'}(M/p) = \operatorname{Pic}^0$ of the function field of $X_{H'}(M/p)$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, where $H'$ is the image of $H$ in $(\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$, onto the sections of $\Lambda.f$ over the generic point, and a bijection $\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}$ from $\operatorname{Pic}^0$ of the $q$-expansion function field at level $\Gamma_{H}(p,M)$ over the residue field of $A$ onto the sections of $\Lambda.f$ over the residue point composed with $\Lambda.\sigma_A$. Let $O$ be a level-$\Gamma_H(M)$ Néron object at $p$ over $\Lambda$, carrying a scheme $O.G$ over the same base with a commutative relative group law, a bijection $O.\mathrm{pts}$ from $J_H(M)$ onto its generic sections compatible with addition and with the Galois action, smoothness, separatedness, local finite type, quasi-compactness, surjectivity and preconnected fibres for $O.g$, Hecke correspondences, flatness and surjectivity of multiplication by positive integers, a toric rank and toric lifts, and the two maps $O.\mathrm{degPts}\, i$ indexed by $i \in \{0,1\}$. Assume: $\Lambda.f$ is smooth and proper with connected fibres and admits a relative group law; $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}$ is additive for $\Lambda.L$; and $\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}$ is additive for the base change of $\Lambda.L$ along the residue point composed with $\Lambda.\sigma_A$, read through the identifications between sections over that base and sections of the base-changed morphism over the identity. Then for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$, every $x \in J_H(M)$ lying in $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^n)$, that is, in the subgroup of $J_H(M)$ generated by the toric points $O.\mathrm{toricPoint}\,(p^n)$ attached to the $A$-algebra maps from the $\mu$-coordinate ring of rank $O.\mathrm{toricRank}$ and exponent $p^n$ to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, and each $i \in \{0,1\}$, one has $O.\mathrm{degPts}\, i\, x = 0$.
--
--   This is the toric clause in Ribet's analysis of the two degeneracy maps at a prime dividing the level exactly once: the toric $p$-power torsion of $J_H(M)$ is annihilated by both degeneracy maps down to level $M/p$. It feeds the results that cut the Tate module of $J_H(M)$ into a $p$-new toric part and a $p$-old finite part, and the computation of Weil pairings on toric points.
